# Commit and push commands

When the user asks for commit or push commands, check the working tree before writing them. Do not run `git commit` or `git push` unless they explicitly ask you to run those commands.

1. Run `git status` and `git check-ignore` on `.env`, `.env.local`, and `supabase/seed.sql`.
2. If a file with a real key, service-role key, or Auth user id is not ignored, add it to `.gitignore` before giving the commands. `supabase/seed.example.sql` is the commitable seed. `supabase/seed.sql` is local.
3. Give commands that cannot add those files. `git add .` is acceptable only after `git status` shows they are ignored and not staged.
4. Tell the user which paths the commit will include, and name any ignored secret file that must stay off the remote.
5. Never print secret values, and never suggest `git add -f` on an ignored secret.

```powershell
# Safe after the ignore check passes
git add .
git status
git commit -m "why this change exists"
git push
```
